.TH BOSWAS-SHELL 1 "November 2026" "Boswas OS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
boswas-shell \- the Boswas OS desktop shell, lock screen and login screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/libexec/boswas/boswas-shell
[\fB\-\-lock\fR | \fB\-\-greeter\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-shell
draws the parts of the desktop that are not application windows. It is
started by
.BR boswas-compositor (1),
which gives it a trusted Wayland connection; it is not meant to be started
by hand. It is an ordinary, unprivileged process: if it exits, the
compositor starts it again and open windows are not affected.
.TP
(no option)
The desktop:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the background, with the desktop icons on the primary output: the files and
folders of the desktop folder (\fBXDG_DESKTOP_DIR\fR of
\fIuser\-dirs.dirs\fR, else \fI~/Desktop\fR) and the system icons chosen
in Settings (Home, Trash, This device), on a grid from the top-left, column
by column. A click selects, Ctrl and Shift add, a drag on the background
selects with a band, a double-click or Enter opens (folders in Files, files
with their default application), a drag moves icons (onto a folder: into
it; onto the Trash: to the Trash), F2 renames, Delete moves to the Trash,
Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X and Ctrl+V copy, cut and paste in the format Files uses,
Ctrl+A selects everything, the arrow keys move and typing goes to an icon
by its name. The background's menu has View (icon size, auto arrange,
align to grid, show desktop icons; written to the user's
\fIcompositor.toml\fR), Sort by (name, size, type, date modified),
Refresh, Paste, New (folder, text document), Open in Terminal and the
settings; an icon's menu has Open, Open with, Cut, Copy, Rename, Delete and
Properties (Files, with the item selected). Files dragged from another
program are copied into the folder (moved when that program asks for a
move). A launcher (\fI.desktop\fR file) on the desktop is started only
when it is a regular file of the user with the execute bit set ("Allow
running this file as a program" in Files); any other launcher opens as text
in the default text editor, and a notice says why. The folder is watched
and read, and files are changed, on worker threads; nothing is done
through a symbolic link;
.IP \(bu 2
the widgets board (Super+W, or the widgets button at the left end of the
taskbar, beside the tray when the taskbar's group is at the left; the
button shows the weather of the lock screen's summary while there is one):
cards for the weather, the next events of Calendar (its own file, with
recurring events expanded), Clock's world clocks and next alarm, the system
(processor and memory, sampled from \fI/proc\fR about once a second only
while the board is open; space on the home file system; the battery), what
is playing (with play, pause and the next track) and the newest picture in
Pictures. A card opens its application; its menu changes its size (small
or medium), moves it or removes it, and "Add widgets" brings a card back.
Nothing is fetched from the network;
.IP \(bu 2
the taskbar along the bottom edge: Start, Task View and the pinned and
running applications in the centre (or at the left edge); tray icons, the
keyboard layout, the network, volume and battery icons, and the clock at the
right. A click on an application shows its window, starts it, or goes
through its windows; the secondary button opens its menu (new window, pin or
unpin, the application's own actions, close); the middle button starts
another instance. Resting the pointer on a running application shows its
windows. A button can show how many notifications its application has in
the notification centre. The taskbar can hide itself: it then leaves the
whole screen to the windows and comes back when the pointer touches the
bottom edge, and while Start, a flyout, a menu or a preview is open;
.IP \(bu 2
Start: search (applications, pages of Boswas Control Center, and running a
command) over one scrolling page with the pinned applications (the first
rows; "Show all" shows every pin), recommendations (applications installed
in the last two weeks and the applications used most) and all applications
by category, in a grid or in a list under letter headings (a letter opens
an index of letters); the user and power menus, and the folders chosen
in Settings next to the power button. Start grows with the screen: eight
columns of pins where the screen is at least 1600x900 logical pixels, else
six, and up to 85 % of the screen's height. Search is local and ranks
applications by how often they were started. Signing out, restarting and
shutting down ask first when windows are open;
.IP \(bu 2
quick settings: Wi-Fi with the list of networks, Bluetooth with the list of
devices, airplane mode, night light, the power mode, do not disturb, media
controls, brightness, and volume with the choice of the sound output;
.IP \(bu 2
notifications: up to three at a time above the taskbar, and the
notification centre with a calendar. While do not disturb is on only
critical notifications pop up; it can turn itself on during set hours. An
application whose banners are turned off reaches the notification centre
only. Nothing pops up while the session is locked;
.IP \(bu 2
the on-screen display of the volume and brightness keys, the Run dialog,
screen capture (an area or the whole screen, saved to the Screenshots
folder under Pictures and copied to the clipboard; a click on its
notification opens the picture, its button the folder), and the dialog that
asks for a password when polkit needs one (with a choice of administrator
when several may authorise the action);
.IP \(bu 2
the clipboard history (Super+V): the last 25 things copied (text, rich
text as its text, pictures up to 8 MB, lists of files), newest first, each
with Pin and Delete, Clear all (pinned items stay) and a search. Enter or a
click pastes an item into the window that had the keyboard: the item goes
on the clipboard and the compositor gives the window Ctrl+V (Ctrl+Shift+V
in a terminal). The arrow keys choose, Delete removes, Ctrl+P pins, typing
searches. The history is kept in memory only and is gone when the session
ends, except pinned items (see FILES). It never keeps what a program marks
as secret (password managers set \fBx\-kde\-passwordManagerHint\fR, and the
concealed and transient markers of other systems), nothing copied while the
session is locked, and nothing at all when \fB[clipboard] history\fR is
false; the lock screen and the login screen do not watch the clipboard;
.IP \(bu 2
the emoji and symbols panel (Super+. or Super+;): emoji by category with a
choice of skin tone, kaomoji and symbols, a search by name and keyword, and
the recently used ones. Enter or a click inserts the item into the window
that had the keyboard in the same way as a paste, and then the clipboard
holds again what it held before (what was on it is copied into memory for
that moment only). Emoji are drawn by the colour emoji font (Noto Color
Emoji); without it the text fonts draw what they can;
.IP \(bu 2
the question "Allow \fIprogram\fR to see your screen?" when a screenshot
tool, a recorder or screen sharing asks the compositor for pictures of the
screen. Neither answer is the default, and Enter alone allows nothing.
While a program may capture, a red mark on the taskbar says which; a click
on it stops the capture.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-lock
The lock screen. It shows the clock and, after a key or a click, asks for
the password of the user who owns the session, checked through PAM (service
\fBboswas\-lock\fR). The session is unlocked only after PAM accepts the
password. It shows no notifications and offers no way to restart or shut
down. Under the clock it shows the weather of the Weather application's
home place, read from a summary file (see FILES), unless
\fB[lock] show_weather\fR in
.BR boswas-compositor (1)'s
configuration is false; it never uses the network itself.
.TP
.B \-\-greeter
The login screen, run by
.BR greetd (1).
It lists the people who can sign in (regular accounts with a login shell)
and accepts another user name. After a successful login greetd starts
.BR boswas-session (1)
for the user.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH KEYBOARD
Every flyout and dialog can be used without a pointer: Tab and Shift+Tab go
through the controls, the arrow keys move to the nearest control in that
direction, Enter or Space activates it, Escape closes. In Start, typing
always searches, the arrow keys go through the whole page (it scrolls along),
Page Up, Page Down, Home and End scroll it; in menus the arrow keys move and
open submenus. The
shortcuts that open things (Super, Super+A, Super+N, Super+W, Super+R,
Super+V, Super+., Print and so on) belong to the compositor and are listed in
.BR boswas-compositor (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/boswas/shell.toml
The applications pinned to the taskbar (\fB[taskbar] pinned\fR) and to
Start (\fB[start] pinned\fR), as lists of desktop file ids, and how Start
lists all applications (\fB[start] view\fR: "category", "grid" or
"list"), and the cards of the widgets board (\fB[widgets] cards\fR, in
order, from "weather", "calendar", "clock", "system", "media" and
"photos"; \fB[widgets] small\fR, the cards shown small). Written by the
shell when something is pinned or unpinned, the view is chosen or the board
is arranged; it may be edited by hand. Without a list the defaults apply:
Files, Firefox ESR and the Software Center on the taskbar, every card on
the board.
.PP
The look and the settings of the taskbar, Start and notifications are part
of the compositor's configuration (\fB[appearance]\fR, \fB[accessibility]\fR,
\fB[taskbar]\fR, \fB[start]\fR, \fB[multitasking]\fR, \fB[desktop]\fR,
\fB[notifications]\fR and \fB[clipboard]\fR in
.BR boswas-compositor (1)),
which passes them on when it starts the shell and when its files are
read again.
.PP
Files and folders the shell opens (a screenshot, the folders next to the
power button) open with their default application: the
\fBmimeapps.list\fR files of the freedesktop MIME applications
specification (\fBboswas\-mimeapps.list\fR before \fBmimeapps.list\fR in
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR, \fI$XDG_CONFIG_DIRS\fR and the
\fIapplications\fR directories of \fI$XDG_DATA_HOME\fR and
\fI$XDG_DATA_DIRS\fR), then the desktop entries that declare the type;
\fBxdg\-open\fR only when none is found.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/launcher\-usage.json
How often each application was started; used to rank search results and
for the "Recommended" list. It never leaves the device.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/run\-history.json
The last commands typed into the Run dialog.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/notification\-senders.json
The applications that sent notifications (name, icon, desktop entry and
when they last did), most recent first, at most 100: Settings lists them to
turn their banners off.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/apps\-seen.json
When each installed application was first seen, for "Recently added". The
applications installed when the shell first ran do not count as new.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/clipboard\-pinned.json
The pinned items of the clipboard history (or under \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME\fR):
text, lists of files and pictures of at most 256 KB, at most 25 items and
4 MB. Readable by the user only (mode 0600, in a directory of mode 0700),
replaced as a whole when an item is pinned, unpinned or deleted, and read
only when it is a regular file within those limits; entries that are not
as written are skipped.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/emoji.json
The emoji, kaomoji and symbols used recently (at most 40) and the chosen
skin tone (mode 0600). Only items of the panel's own list are read back.
.PP
When the history is off and Turn on is chosen in its flyout, the shell
sets \fB[clipboard] history = true\fR in
.I ~/.config/boswas/compositor.toml
(the one line, or the table added; the file is left alone when the result
could not be read back for certain, and Settings opens instead) and asks
the compositor to read its files again.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/weather.json
The weather summary the lock screen shows (or under
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME\fR), written by
.BR boswas-weather (1)
and its background update; it is read only if it is a regular file owned by
the user, at most 16 KB, and not older than three hours.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/boswas/desktop\-layout.json
Where desktop icons were put by hand (in grid cells) and the order chosen
with Sort by, while auto arrange is off. Anything in it that is not
understood is left out.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/com.boswas.Calendar/calendar.json
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/com.boswas.Clock/clock.json
Read (never written) for the widgets board, when they are regular files of
the user of a sensible size (8 MB and 4 MB at most); time zones come from
\fI/usr/share/zoneinfo\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/Trash
Where Delete on the desktop moves items (the freedesktop Trash
specification); an item on another drive is not moved.
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/boswas\-lock
PAM service of the lock screen.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BOSWAS_LOG
Log filter (for example \fBdebug\fR). Messages go to standard error.
.TP
.B GREETD_SOCK
Set by greetd for the login screen.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the shell was asked to end. 2 for a wrong option. 1 on an error,
with one line on standard error that says what was wrong: the compositor could not be reached, or no
font is installed, so that nothing could be read on the screen. The fonts
are looked for through fontconfig and, when that finds none, in
.IR /usr/share/fonts ,
.I /usr/local/share/fonts
and the user's own font directories. The lock screen is the exception: it
stays without text and writes the error to its log, because ending it
would leave a locked session that cannot be unlocked.
.PP
The compositor starts a desktop shell again when it ends, and gives up on
a login screen that keeps ending; see
.BR boswas-compositor (1).
.SH SECURITY
The shell runs as the user, never as root. Programs it starts get neither
its trusted Wayland connection nor its control connection to the
compositor. Run starts a program with its arguments; there is no shell and
nothing is expanded. Passwords and Wi-Fi passphrases are wiped from memory
after use. Wallpapers, icons, window previews and what tray items send are
decoded in the shell with size limits. The clipboard is read through
clipboard control, which the compositor offers to the programs it starts
itself and to no application; each transfer has a size and a time limit,
and what the shell puts on the clipboard is marked so that it does not
take it in again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas-compositor (1),
.BR boswas-session (1),
.BR boswas-weather (1)
